---
scope: src/tools/init
description: >
  Bootstrap tool that turns a cold project into an AIDE-ready project as fast as
  possible — writes non-existing files silently, only pausing to ask the user
  when a category would overwrite an existing file or when an inherently
  interactive decision (brain vault path, MCP config merge, IDE choice) is
  required.
intent: >
  aide_init is the bootstrap — the one command that turns a cold
  project into an AIDE-ready project. It is designed to be fast and
  unobtrusive: when the category has nothing on disk to overwrite, it
  just writes the files and reports what it did. The user is only
  interrupted when init would destroy or clobber something they
  already have, or when a decision cannot be resolved from the
  filesystem alone.

  The rule is: confirmation is reserved for overwrites and for
  inherent decisions. A category whose plan contains only would-create
  steps runs silently and reports its results. A category whose plan
  contains would-overwrite steps pauses and asks the user — category-
  by-category, using the same wizard shape as before — before
  applying. Inherent decisions — framework detection confirmation,
  brain vault location, MCP server-merge into an existing config, and
  IDE choice — always prompt the user regardless of disk state,
  because filesystem contents cannot resolve them.

  The categories are: framework detection, methodology pointer stub,
  methodology doc hub, pipeline slash commands, pipeline agent
  definitions, pipeline skill templates, MCP server registration
  (aide), brain vault provisioning, MCP server registration
  (obsidian), and IDE configuration. Each category still reports what
  it would do (create, overwrite, skip, already exists), and the
  agent only surfaces the category to the user when an overwrite is
  pending or an inherent decision is needed.

  The brain layer is not optional — AIDE is opinionated about this.
  The pipeline needs a brain to persist research and retros. But the
  user chooses where it lives: they can connect to an existing vault
  anywhere on their system, or have init create a new one at a
  location they specify. The agent interviews the user to resolve the
  vault path; the tool never silently assumes a sibling folder or
  falls back to a default. This interview happens regardless of disk
  state because the vault path is an inherent decision, not a
  file-write decision.

  MCP config changes are returned as prescriptions — the key name and
  server entry the config needs — not written directly by the tool.
  The agent reads the existing MCP config, merges the prescribed entry
  into it, and writes the result. The user sees the merge before it
  happens. This ensures the agent has full visibility into what exists
  in the config and the user's other servers are never at risk, even
  when the config is malformed.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The tool returns a structured JSON response containing a
      categorized list of steps, each with a status (would-create,
      would-overwrite, would-skip, exists) and the data the agent
      needs to execute that step (file paths, content, MCP entries).
      The agent interprets this response and decides per-category
      whether user interaction is needed.
    - Framework auto-detection picks the right framework from files
      on disk, and the agent confirms the detection with the user
      before proceeding. Framework choice is an inherent decision —
      the prompt happens regardless of disk state.
    - "A category whose plan contains only would-create steps (no
      existing files on disk) is applied silently by the agent: no
      AskUserQuestion, no confirmation, just write and report. The
      bootstrap is intentionally fast for the common cold-start path
      where there is nothing to preserve."
    - "A category whose plan contains at least one would-overwrite
      step pauses the agent category-by-category using
      AskUserQuestion with structured options (not conversational
      questions). The agent names the files that would be
      overwritten so the user can make an informed choice, then
      either applies the whole category or skips it per the user's
      selection."
    - "Inherent decisions — brain vault location, MCP server merge
      into an existing config, and IDE choice — always prompt the
      user regardless of what is on disk. These are not file-write
      decisions; filesystem state cannot resolve them. The fast-path
      rule applies only to pure file-create categories."
    - For MCP config, the tool returns a prescription — the server
      key name and the entry object — instead of writing the file.
      The agent reads the existing config, shows the user the
      planned merge, and writes. If the config is malformed JSON,
      the agent tells the user and asks how to proceed rather than
      silently clobbering.
    - "The brain vault interview is agent-driven and always happens.
      The agent asks the user where their vault is, offering three
      paths: discover an existing vault (from env var, known
      locations, or user input), create a new vault at a specified
      location, or connect to an existing vault at a user-provided
      path. The vault is always provisioned — there is no skip-brain
      option. Once the path is resolved, the tool reports whether
      the vault needs scaffolding and the agent applies it."
    - "Wiring the brain end-to-end from a resolved brainPath requires
      callers to invoke aide_init twice with distinct category
      values: once with \"category: mcp\" to apply the obsidian MCP
      prescription to .mcp.json, and once with \"category: brain\"
      to scaffold vault directories and seed content files. The
      split is intentional and load-bearing: the mcp category is
      prescription-only (the agent merges the returned entry into
      .mcp.json because the tool never writes that file directly),
      while the brain category is direct filesystem work (the tool
      creates vault directories and seeds content itself). Neither
      category alone fully wires the brain — both must run for the
      brain to be reachable by pipeline agents."
    - IDE configuration is presented as optional — the agent asks
      whether the user wants Zed and/or VS Code configuration, and
      only applies what they confirm. IDE choice is an inherent
      decision and always prompts.
    - Every step is idempotent. Re-running init on a fully
      initialized project returns a plan where every step shows
      "exists" and the agent reports that nothing needs to change —
      without asking a single question.
    - The tool's response is structured data the agent interprets,
      not a pre-formatted summary the agent parrots. The agent
      composes its own conversational output from the structured
      data, tailored to the user's context.
    - The default response (no category parameter) is small enough
      for an agent to comprehend in a single read — metadata only,
      no file content — and includes overwrite status per category
      so the agent can decide which categories need prompts.
    - When called with a `category` parameter, the tool writes all
      `would-create` files directly to disk itself and returns a
      small manifest — an array of steps with `filePath`, `status`
      (now `"created"`, `"overwritten"`, or `"exists"`), and `name`,
      but NO `content` field. The agent never sees file content and
      never needs to use the Write tool for new files. This
      eliminates the 50-100KB JSON response problem that caused
      agents to resort to programmatic parsing via Node scripts
      instead of conversational interaction.
    - The agent only uses the Write tool for files that require
      intelligent merging — specifically MCP config, where the
      agent must read the existing config, merge prescriptions,
      and write. For MCP steps, the category call still returns
      the prescription data (not file content) so the agent can
      perform the merge. Brain vault scaffolding (creating
      directories) is also handled by the tool directly.
    - IDE steps that require external tooling (e.g., VS Code
      extension install via CLI) return instructions for the agent
      to execute, since those aren't simple file writes.
  undesired:
    - An init that prompts the user when there is nothing to
      overwrite. A category whose plan is entirely would-create
      steps must be applied silently — asking permission to write
      files that do not yet exist is the exact friction this
      intent shift removes.
    - An init that silently overwrites existing files without
      asking. The fast path is for creates only; overwrites are
      destructive and must pause the agent so the user can
      consent category-by-category.
    - "A category that bundles creates and overwrites and silently
      applies both because most of its files are new. If any file
      in the category would overwrite, the category pauses — the
      user is informed about the overwrite and decides whether to
      apply the whole category."
    - An init that asks conversational questions instead of using
      AskUserQuestion with structured options for the overwrite
      and inherent-decision paths. Every pause point must use
      AskUserQuestion with defined labels the user can select —
      not open-ended text prompts.
    - An init that skips the inherent-decision prompts (brain
      vault path, MCP config merge, IDE choice) because the
      fast-path rule was misapplied. Those prompts are not about
      file-writes and must always happen.
    - An init that silently assumes a brain vault path from a
      sibling folder, environment variable, or platform default
      without asking the user. Discovery hints are useful for
      suggesting a default, but the agent must confirm the path
      with the user.
    - An init that writes MCP config entries directly, bypassing
      the agent. The prescription pattern exists so the agent has
      full visibility and the user's existing servers are never
      at risk.
    - An init that allows skipping the brain entirely. AIDE
      requires a brain — research and retros depend on it. The
      user chooses where, not whether.
    - "A caller invoking only \"category: brain\" with a resolved
      brainPath and declaring the brain fully wired. That single
      invocation scaffolds the vault directories and seeds content
      but leaves the obsidian MCP prescription unapplied — the
      .mcp.json vaultPath stays empty, pipeline agents that declare
      mcpServers: [obsidian] cannot reach the vault, and aide_info
      will still report \"brain.status: invalid-path\" even though
      the vault exists on disk. The symmetric failure also applies:
      a caller invoking only \"category: mcp\" with a brainPath
      patches .mcp.json but never scaffolds the vault, leaving the
      obsidian server pointed at an unprovisioned directory. Both
      categories must run; treating either alone as sufficient
      produces a half-wired brain."
    - An init that clobbers the MCP config when it encounters
      malformed JSON. The agent handles this case by asking the
      user, not by overwriting.
    - An init that returns a pre-formatted prose summary instead
      of structured data. The agent needs structured JSON to
      drive the flow — not a string it has to parse or parrot.
    - A category call that returns full file content for the
      agent to write. The category call must write files to disk
      itself and return only a manifest. Returning content causes
      agents to receive 13-50KB responses that get persisted to
      disk as JSON files, which the agent then tries to parse
      with Node scripts instead of using the Write tool per file.
    - An init response that embeds full file content for all
      steps in a single payload. Even when steps are structured,
      a 50-100KB JSON blob causes agents to write Python scripts
      to parse it instead of driving the install interactively.
      Content must be deferred to category-specific follow-up
      calls.
    - An init that appends the pointer stub on every run, fails
      the whole run on a single step failure, hardcodes vault
      paths, or reaches for the network. All prior undesired
      outcomes from the original spec still apply.
    - "A new init category added without a corresponding upgrade
      category. The upgrade surface must mirror the init surface —
      every category init installs must have an upgrade path. The
      two category lists (InitCategory and UpgradeCategory) are
      currently independent; adding to one without the other
      creates silent drift."
---

## Context

Every new project that adopts AIDE has to cross the same cold-start gap:
an entry-point pointer to AIDE in the surface the agent already reads on
every session, the full methodology installed somewhere the agent can
crawl on demand, pipeline slash commands scaffolded, pipeline agent
definitions installed, pipeline skill templates installed, the MCP
server registered, the brain layer provisioned so pipeline agents can
persist and retrieve domain knowledge, and the IDE taught to render
`.aide` as Markdown. Without a single tool that does all of this, each
project rediscovers the list and gets one or two steps wrong — a stub
written to the wrong file, a doc hub installed to a path the stub
doesn't name, a slash command with a typo, an MCP config that
registers the server under the wrong name, no vault for the research
agent to write to, or an IDE that still renders `.aide` as plain text.
This tool exists to collapse the cold-start gap into one command, so
the decision to adopt AIDE in a new project is trivially reversible
and costs nothing to try.

The previous agent-driven design corrected the biggest failure mode of
the original monolithic tool — silent overwrites of malformed MCP
configs, vaults silently created at sibling defaults — by pausing the
agent category-by-category and asking the user to confirm each one
before writing. That was the right instinct for destructive writes. It
was the wrong tax for creates. The observed friction in cold-start
runs is a user watching the agent ask "can I create `.aide/docs/`?
can I create `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`? can I create
`.claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md`?" for seven consecutive
categories of files that do not yet exist — nothing to preserve,
nothing to clobber, no decision the user can meaningfully make beyond
"yes, I ran init, apply it." Every one of those prompts is
bureaucratic overhead on a path where the user has already committed.

This spec refines the confirmation model by splitting init's decisions
into two kinds. **File-write decisions** are resolvable from disk
state: the tool can stat every target path and know with certainty
whether a given category would create new files, overwrite existing
ones, or do nothing. When every step in a category would create
(nothing on disk, nothing to preserve), the category has no decision
the user can inform — the right answer is to apply it silently.
**Inherent decisions** are the ones disk state cannot resolve:
framework choice (two frameworks can coexist on disk and the user
alone knows which one the project actually targets), brain vault
location (the user is the only one who knows where their knowledge
lives), whether to merge the AIDE servers into their existing MCP
config (the user's other servers are in that file), and which IDEs
they actually want configured (tooling they don't use is noise). These
always prompt, regardless of what is on disk, because the question
cannot be answered by reading the filesystem.

The shift is behavioral, not architectural. The tool still plans every
step, still returns structured data, still executes writes itself via
apply mode when called with a category — nothing about the two-call
pattern changes. What changes is the agent's branch logic over the
plan: a category whose plan is entirely would-create steps is applied
silently and reported after the fact; a category whose plan contains
at least one would-overwrite step pauses the agent with the affected
files named so the user can make an informed choice about their own
existing work; inherent decisions always prompt because they are not
file-write decisions at all. A fully initialized project, re-run,
produces a plan that is entirely `exists` steps, zero prompts, and a
terse "nothing to change" report. A fresh project produces a plan
that is entirely `would-create` file steps plus three-or-four inherent
prompts, and the user is interrupted only for those.

Methodology delivery inside this tool is split across two peer
helpers on purpose. One installs the short pointer stub into the
host's config file so the agent learns in its very first read of the
session that AIDE exists and where the full material lives. The other
installs the full material — the canonical methodology docs plus a
hub index — into a dedicated doc-hub folder on the host's disk, so
the agent reads only what the current task requires and only when the
task requires it. The split mirrors the methodology's own
progressive-disclosure rule at the delivery layer: the high-traffic
surface teaches where to look, the low-traffic surface holds the
actual content. Both land through the same silent-on-create path when
nothing of the user's is at risk.

The brain layer is the pipeline's shared memory. The research agent
writes domain findings to `research/<domain>/`, QA promotes retros to
`process/retro/`, and the coding playbook lives under
`coding-playbook/`. Without a vault and an Obsidian MCP server entry,
agents that declare `mcpServers: [obsidian]` in their frontmatter
silently lose brain access — their research vanishes, retros are
never persisted, and the pipeline cannot learn across runs. Init
provisions this layer: it creates a minimal vault with the expected
directory scaffolding when the user-chosen location is empty, and
prescribes the Obsidian MCP server entry for the agent to merge into
the project's MCP config alongside the aide server. The vault path is
resolved through the agent's interview with the user — never
hardcoded, never silently defaulted — because the vault path is an
inherent decision and the rule that covers it is "always prompt," not
"apply silently when nothing is on disk."

## Strategy

**The tool plans with a four-status enum; the agent branches on
status distribution per category.** Each step in the plan reports
`would-create` (target path does not exist), `would-overwrite`
(target path exists and the canonical bytes differ from what is on
disk), `would-skip` (target path exists and the canonical bytes
match, so no write is needed), or `exists` (step is already
satisfied, nothing to do). The agent walks the plan category by
category and picks one of three branches: if the category's steps
are all `would-create` (or a mix of `would-create`, `would-skip`,
and `exists`), apply silently — there is no decision the user can
inform, and every prompt on that path is friction the spec exists to
remove. If any step in the category is `would-overwrite`, pause and
ask, naming the files so the user can decide whether to overwrite
their own modified material. This is how the spec's outcomes.desired
rule "a pure-create category is applied silently; a category with
overwrites pauses" cashes out in agent-visible behavior.

**`would-overwrite` is computed by byte comparison, not heuristics.**
Each helper stats its target path; if the file exists, it reads the
on-disk bytes and compares them to the canonical content it was about
to write. Identical bytes ⇒ `would-skip` (the file is already what we
would have written, no need to write again). Different bytes ⇒
`would-overwrite` (the user has modified this file, or a prior
version of the tool wrote something that has since drifted). Missing
file ⇒ `would-create`. The rule is deliberately strict: "same name
but different content" is enough to pause the agent, because the
tool cannot tell a user-authored customization from a stale previous
install, and the safer branch is to ask. This closes the
outcomes.undesired line "a category that bundles creates and
overwrites and silently applies both because most of its files are
new" — even one overwrite in a category tips the whole category into
the prompt branch.

**Inherent-decision prompts are orthogonal to the file-write
branch.** Framework confirmation, brain vault location, MCP-config
merge into an existing config, and IDE choice always pause the agent
regardless of the plan's status distribution. The rule "silent on
create, prompt on overwrite" governs file-writes only; inherent
decisions are not file-writes, they are choices about what the user
wants their environment to look like, and the filesystem cannot
resolve them. A fully-initialized project re-run still asks zero
questions because the categories land as `exists` *and* the inherent
decisions have already been answered on disk — brain path present in
the registered MCP server entry, IDE config present, framework
already detected — so the agent has no new questions to ask. A fresh
project asks three or four (framework confirm, brain vault path, MCP
merge approval, IDE choice) and nothing else. This closes the
outcomes.undesired line "an init that skips the inherent-decision
prompts because the fast-path rule was misapplied" — the rule does
not apply to them in the first place.

**Apply mode writes creates immediately and defers overwrites to
user consent.** When the agent calls init with a category parameter,
the tool's apply helper walks the category's steps: every
`would-create` step is written to disk straight through and returned
with status `"created"`; `would-skip` and `exists` steps pass through
unchanged; `would-overwrite` steps are only written when the agent
explicitly signaled user approval for the category (the agent does
this by proceeding to the apply call — if the user had declined,
the agent would never have made it). On approval, the overwrite
happens and the step is returned with status `"overwritten"`. The
manifest that comes back carries the new `"overwritten"` status
alongside `"created"` and `"exists"` so the agent can report a
truthful breakdown — "created 8, overwrote 1, kept 3 as-is" — not a
vague "done." This satisfies the outcomes.desired line about manifest
statuses and the outcomes.undesired line about silent clobbers
(writes happen, but only when the plan already showed them as
overwrites and the agent already got approval).

**Category isolation: one category's decision never mutates
another.** Each helper (methodology, commands, agents, skills, MCP,
brain, IDE) produces its plan from its own canonical content and its
own disk inspection. No shared state, no cross-category coupling, no
ordering dependency beyond the agent's presentation order. This
matches the orchestrator/helper pattern the codebase already uses
(`study-playbook` convention: orchestrator coordinates, helpers do
one thing) and is the forcing function that makes per-category
pauses work — the agent can apply methodology silently, pause on
commands because the user has a modified slash command, apply agents
silently, and the categories do not contaminate each other. A
category's failure surfaces as a single category's failure in the
report; it does not abort the rest. This closes the outcomes.desired
"every step is idempotent" line at the category-boundary level too.

**MCP merge is an inherent-decision wrapper around file-write
prescription.** The tool never writes MCP config. It returns the
server key name and the entry object the config needs; the agent
reads the existing config, shows the user the planned merge, and
writes. The "show the user before writing" pause is an
inherent-decision prompt — the user's other servers live in that
file, the tool cannot know which ones the user wants preserved, and
the filesystem state of `.mcp.json` does not answer the question.
Malformed JSON hits the same path: the agent surfaces the parse
error and asks how to proceed rather than silently replacing the
file. This closes the outcomes.undesired lines about clobbering MCP
configs and writing MCP entries directly, and honors the parent
spec's `src/.aide` invariant that the tool never touches MCP config.

**Brain vault is required; location is inherent; scaffolding is
silent-on-create.** AIDE is opinionated that the pipeline needs
persistent memory. The agent asks the user where their vault is,
offering discovered hints (env var, sibling `my-brain/`, prior
install) as suggestions the user can pick or override — the user
cannot skip the vault, only choose where it lives. Once the path is
resolved, the brain category's scaffolding plan follows the normal
file-write branch: if the expected directories are absent, the tool
creates them silently; if the vault exists and the scaffolding is
intact, everything shows `exists` and no writes happen. The inherent
prompt (where?) and the silent-on-create write (create the
directories once the path is known) are cleanly separated. This
closes the outcomes.undesired lines about skipping the brain and
about silently adopting a sibling default.

**Framework detection is a read-only inspection whose result is
always confirmed.** Framework choice is inherent — two frameworks
can coexist on disk, and the user alone knows which one matters for
this project. The detector returns its best guess and the agent
confirms with the user; the user can accept or override. Detection
never writes. The confirmation prompt happens even when the
detection is unambiguous because the user is the source of truth
about their own project, not the presence of a `.claude/`
directory.

**IDE configuration is per-IDE and always asked.** The user's IDE
preferences are inherent — a developer on VS Code has no use for a
Zed settings file, and vice versa. The agent asks which IDEs to
configure; it only applies what the user confirms. IDE steps that
need external tooling (VS Code extension install via CLI) return an
`instructions` field the agent can execute rather than attempting
the shell call from inside the tool, because the tool has no
guarantee the CLI is on PATH and "the rest of init succeeds when IDE
fails" requires that IDE failure be reportable, not crashing.

**Network is never the happy path.** Every asset init needs is
checked into the repo — the methodology docs, the command
templates, the agent definitions, the skill templates, the bundled
VS Code `.vsix`. A developer running init on a laptop with no
network gets the same result as a developer running it online, and
the silent-on-create branch is safe because the canonical content
is local and deterministic.

**The upgrade surface must mirror the init surface, category for
category.** Every new init category has a corresponding upgrade
category, and the two lists are maintained together. The
outcomes.undesired line naming drift between `InitCategory` and
`UpgradeCategory` is a deliberate guardrail — a category installed
by init but not tracked by upgrade is silently frozen at install
time, and a category upgraded without an install counterpart is
unreachable on cold start. Keep them in lockstep.

## Good examples

**Cold start on a fresh project — three prompts, nothing else.** The
agent calls `aide_init` with no category. The plan comes back with
every step in `methodology`, `commands`, `agents`, `skills`, and the
`.aide/` tree as `would-create`; MCP returns its prescription and the
brain slot returns its placeholder because no path is resolved yet;
IDE returns its `would-create` steps.

The agent opens with:

> I detected Claude Code. Is that right?

The user confirms. The agent, seeing every file category is pure
`would-create`, applies methodology, commands, agents, skills, and
`.aide/` tree one after another without another prompt, reporting
briefly after each:

> Installed methodology — 9 files under `.aide/docs/` and a pointer stub in `CLAUDE.md`.
> Installed 12 slash commands under `.claude/commands/aide/`.
> Installed 6 pipeline agent definitions under `.claude/agents/aide/`.
> Installed study-playbook skill under `.claude/skills/`.
> Scaffolded `.aide/intent.aide` for the project root.

Then the inherent prompts:

> AIDE needs a brain vault. I found a candidate at `D:/Code/Me/my-brain/` (sibling folder) and one at `$BRAIN` (env var → `D:/Notes/main-vault/`). Pick one, or type a path.

The user picks the env-var hint. The agent calls init with
`category: "brain"` and that path; the tool scaffolds the missing
directories and returns `"created"` for each. Then:

> Your `.mcp.json` has `github` and `memory` configured. I'll add `aide` and `obsidian` alongside them. OK?

The user confirms; the agent merges and writes. Then:

> I can configure Zed and VS Code for `.aide` preview. Want both, one, or neither?

The user picks Zed only; agent applies the Zed step silently, skips
VS Code. Total prompts from a cold start: four (framework, brain
path, MCP merge, IDE choice). Zero prompts about file-creates.

**Re-run on a fully initialized project — zero prompts.** The
developer runs `npx aidemd-mcp init` a week later. Every file category comes
back entirely as `exists`. The MCP config already contains the aide
and obsidian servers. The brain scaffold is intact. The agent
reports once and stops:

> This project is already fully initialized — methodology, commands, agents, skills, `.aide` tree, MCP servers, brain vault, and Zed preview all present. Nothing to change.

No AskUserQuestion fires. The agent asked nothing because the plan
showed nothing to decide.

**Partial overwrite — one file named, one prompt, one category.**
The developer has edited `.claude/commands/aide/research.md` to add
a line of their own instructions. A release later they re-run init.
The plan comes back with the `commands` category mixed: eleven
`would-skip` steps (canonical bytes match), one `would-overwrite`
step for `research.md`. Every other category is pure `would-create`
or pure `exists`.

The agent applies methodology silently (all `would-create`), applies
agents silently, applies skills silently. At commands it pauses:

> The `commands` category has one file that would overwrite your existing version: `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`. Eleven other slash commands are either already current or missing and would be freshly installed. Overwrite your modified `research.md`?

Options: "Overwrite it" / "Keep my version and install the others" /
"Skip the whole category." The user picks the middle option. The
agent calls init with `category: "commands"` and signals that the
overwrite is declined; the tool writes only the `would-create`
steps, returns the `would-overwrite` step with status unchanged, and
the agent reports:

> Installed 11 new slash commands under `.claude/commands/aide/`. Kept your modified `research.md` as-is.

One prompt, surgically scoped to the one file that actually needed a
decision.

## Bad examples

**Prompting on pure-create categories.** Cold start, fresh project,
and the agent asks:

> Methodology — this will create the CLAUDE.md pointer stub and 9 docs in `.aide/docs/`. Want me to set this up?

Then:

> Commands — this will create 12 slash commands in `.claude/commands/aide/`. Want me to set these up?

Then:

> Agents — this will create 6 pipeline agents in `.claude/agents/aide/`. Want me to set these up?

The user hits "yes" seven times in a row for files that do not yet
exist, staring at a wizard that is asking permission to do the thing
they invoked it to do. This is the exact friction the new
confirmation model removes; the category-by-category pause belongs
to overwrites, not creates.

**Silent overwrite because "most of the category is new."** A
developer has a customized `research.md` from the previous example.
The agent heuristically decides the `commands` category is "mostly
creates (eleven new, one overwrite)" and applies the whole category
without pausing, overwriting the user's modifications. The agent
reports "installed 12 slash commands" and the user discovers
tomorrow that their custom instructions are gone. The rule is
strict: even one `would-overwrite` step in a category tips the
category into the prompt branch.

**Skipping an inherent-decision prompt because the "fast path"
applies.** The agent sees a `my-brain/` directory sibling to the
project, decides that counts as the brain being "already present,"
skips the brain-path question, and wires the project's MCP config
to that sibling vault. The user's actual AIDE vault is at
`D:/Notes/aide-vault/` and the sibling is an old experiment. The
inherent-decision rule exists precisely for this case: the vault
path is not resolvable from disk state, so the agent must ask
regardless of what is on disk.

**Silent MCP merge.** The agent finds `.mcp.json` on disk with two
existing servers, assumes the merge is a file-write decision, and
writes the new config without pausing. In the best case the user is
surprised; in the worst case the existing config was malformed and
the tool has just replaced it with a "clean" version that silently
dropped the user's custom servers. MCP merge is inherent — always
prompt.

**One summary table, one blanket confirmation, zero granularity.**
The agent returns:

> Here's the full plan: methodology (9 creates), commands (12 creates), agents (6 creates), skills (1 create), MCP (1 prescription), brain (pending vault path), IDE (2 creates). Apply all?

The user says yes and loses their modified `research.md` to a silent
overwrite they had no way to see in the table. The per-category
pause model exists so overwrites surface against their own category,
with the affected files named — not buried in a global "apply all."

**Prompts fired via conversational text instead of
AskUserQuestion.** The agent writes:

> Do you want me to go ahead with methodology? (yes/no)

The user types "y" and the agent parses it. This violates the
structured-options rule from outcomes.undesired — every pause point
must use `AskUserQuestion` with defined labels, because the agent is
not a CLI and free-text prompts waste turns on parsing.

**Reporting that does not distinguish created from overwritten.**
After an apply call that wrote six new files and overwrote one, the
agent reports:

> Done — installed 7 files.

The user has no way to know which file was theirs and got replaced.
The manifest statuses exist so the agent can say "created 6,
overwrote 1: research.md" — using them as a single undifferentiated
count defeats the purpose of the new status enum.

